WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Secure input handling depends on failure behavior.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:10.760
Fail closed means the program rejects, stops, or returns a safe error when required checks fail.

3
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:17.740
It does not continue with partially initialized objects, guessed defaults, stale values, or half-parsed state.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:28.080
C++ programs may use error returns, expected-style results, exceptions, or status objects, but the design should make failures visible and hard to ignore.

5
00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:33.540
Ambiguous failure handling is where many validation checks quietly lose their value.

6
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.540
RAII helps defensive code by tying resource cleanup to object lifetime.

7
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:47.120
Files, locks, memory, temporary objects, and handles can be released reliably when a parse fails or an exception is thrown.

8
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.740
Cleanup reliability matters because malformed input often exercises unusual paths.

9
00:00:52.840 --> 00:01:01.640
Logging is useful, but logs should not expose secrets, raw tokens, sensitive paths, or unbounded attacker-controlled text.

10
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:08.380
Error messages should help troubleshooting without turning diagnostics into another disclosure or injection problem.

11
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:26.420
Testing should include edge cases and hostile shapes of input: empty values, maximum lengths, one byte over the limit, malformed encodings, invalid numbers, negative values, huge counts, missing fields, duplicate fields, deeply nested structures, and truncated binary records.

12
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.200
Fuzzing can discover combinations humans would not write by hand.

13
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:41.740
Compiler warnings, sanitizers, static analysis, and code review add more coverage by surfacing memory errors, undefined behavior, suspicious conversions, and unchecked assumptions.

14
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.960
The goal is to prove that rejection paths are as engineered as success paths.
